[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Error Handling in React: Try/Catch and Error Boundaries"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React applications, like any software, are susceptible to errors. Handling these errors gracefully is crucial for providing a good user experience and preventing application crashes. React offers two primary mechanisms for error handling: `try/catch` statements for synchronous code and Error Boundaries for handling errors in React components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Try/Catch Statements"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"`try/catch` statements are a standard JavaScript construct for handling synchronous errors. They allow you to attempt a block of code and, if an error occurs, catch and handle it gracefully. In React, you can use `try/catch` within your component functions to handle errors that might occur during synchronous operations."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function MyComponent() {
  try {
    // Code that might throw an error
    const result = someSynchronousFunction();
    return <div>{result}</div>;
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle the error
    console.error("An error occurred:", error);
    return <div>Error: Something went wrong.</div>;
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `someSynchronousFunction()` might throw an error. If it does, the `catch` block will execute, logging the error to the console and rendering a user-friendly error message.  It's important to note that `try/catch` only works for synchronous code. It won't catch errors that occur within asynchronous operations like `setTimeout`, `fetch`, or within React's component rendering lifecycle."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Boundaries"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error Boundaries are React components that catch JavaScript errors anywhere in their child component tree, log those errors, and display a fallback UI instead of the component tree that crashed. Error Boundaries catch errors during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors of the whole tree below them. They **do not** catch errors for:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event handlers (click events, etc.)"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Asynchronous code (e.g., `setTimeout` or `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks)"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server-side rendering"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Errors thrown in the error boundary itself (rather than its children)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class component becomes an Error Boundary if it defines either (or both) of the lifecycle methods `static getDerivedStateFromError()` or `componentDidCatch()`."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"`static getDerivedStateFromError()`"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"This lifecycle method is used to update the state in response to an error. It receives the error that was thrown as an argument and should return a value to update the state.  Typically, you'll use this to set a flag indicating that an error occurred, which you can then use to render a fallback UI."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"`componentDidCatch()`"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"This lifecycle method is called after an error has been thrown by a descendant component. It receives two arguments:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`error`: The error that was thrown."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`info`: An object containing information about the error, including the component stack trace."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can use `componentDidCatch()` to log the error to an error reporting service or perform other side effects."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    // Update state so the next render will show the fallback UI.
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    // Example "componentStack":
    //   in ComponentThatThrows (created by App)
    //   in App
    console.error("Error caught by ErrorBoundary:", error, info.componentStack);
    // You can also log the error to an error reporting service
    // logErrorToMyService(error, info.componentStack);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      // You can render any custom fallback UI
      return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;
    }

    return this.props.children; 
  }
}

export default ErrorBoundary;"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `ErrorBoundary` catches errors from its child components. If an error occurs, `getDerivedStateFromError` updates the state to indicate that an error has occurred. The `render` method then checks the `hasError` state and renders a fallback UI if necessary.  `componentDidCatch` logs the error and component stack trace to the console. You could replace the console log with a call to a real error reporting service."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Error Boundaries"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use an Error Boundary, simply wrap the components that you want to protect with the Error Boundary component:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import ErrorBoundary from './ErrorBoundary';

function App() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
      <ComponentThatMightThrow />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, if `ComponentThatMightThrow` throws an error during rendering, the `ErrorBoundary` will catch it and render the fallback UI.  You can place Error Boundaries strategically throughout your application to isolate errors and prevent them from crashing the entire application. It is generally a good idea to have a top-level error boundary to catch any unexpected errors that might occur."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Important Considerations"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Error Boundaries only catch errors in the components *below* them in the tree. An Error Boundary can’t catch an error within itself."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The granularity of Error Boundaries is up to you. You can wrap top-level routes to display a generic "Something went wrong" message, or you can wrap individual components to isolate errors more precisely."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Error Boundaries are a powerful tool for improving the resilience of your React applications, but they are not a replacement for careful error handling within your components. Use `try/catch` for synchronous operations and consider using libraries like `Sentry` or `Bugsnag` for more advanced error reporting."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling in Event Handlers"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error boundaries do not catch errors inside event handlers. You will need to use a standard `try/catch` block inside your event handlers."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"function MyComponent() {
  const handleClick = () => {
    try {
      // Code that might throw an error
      doSomethingRisky();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Error in event handler:", error);
      // Optionally, update state to display an error message
      // this.setState({ errorMessage: 'Something went wrong!' });
    }
  };

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click Me</button>;
}"},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining `try/catch` statements for synchronous code and Error Boundaries for component rendering, you can build more robust and user-friendly React applications that handle errors gracefully and prevent unexpected crashes. Remember to strategically place Error Boundaries throughout your application and to handle errors in event handlers with `try/catch` blocks."}]
